const guides=[
{about:"A C++ source file is translated by a compiler into machine code. The language gives you control over types and resources while the standard library supplies reusable tools.",how:"Start with the smallest valid program, identify each translation step, and use compiler diagnostics to narrow down mistakes. A warning is useful information even when compilation succeeds.",code:"#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << \"Hello, C++!\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}",task:"Create a tiny console program, compile it with warnings enabled, then deliberately introduce and fix one syntax error.",q:"What does the compiler do with a C++ source file?",opts:["Translates source code into a form the computer can execute","Runs every line directly without translation","Automatically designs the user interface","Stores the program only as plain text"],a:0},
{about:"Objects store values of specific types. Initialization gives an object its first value, while expressions combine values using operators whose rules determine the result.",how:"Choose a type that represents the domain, initialize it at the point of declaration, and make conversions explicit when precision or range could change.",code:"#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int lives{3};\n    double speed{4.5};\n    const int maxLives{5};\n    lives = maxLives;\n    std::cout << lives << ' ' << speed << '\\n';\n}",task:"Represent a player's score, health, and movement speed. Choose a type for each, initialize them, and calculate a new score.",q:"Why is brace initialization often a good default?",opts:["It can reject narrowing conversions","It always makes a variable constant","It converts every value to int","It skips initialization"],a:0},
{about:"The iostream library provides streams for console input and output. A stream carries data and also records whether an operation succeeded.",how:"Use std::cout for output, std::cin for formatted input, and std::getline when a complete line is required. Check stream state before trusting user input.",code:"#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string name;\n    std::cout << \"Name: \";\n    std::getline(std::cin, name);\n    std::cout << \"Hello, \" << name << '\\n';\n}",task:"Build a greeting program that reads a full name including spaces and prints a formatted response.",q:"Which function reads an entire line including spaces?",opts:["std::getline","std::cin >> name","std::cout","std::ignore only"],a:0},
{about:"Boolean expressions evaluate to true or false. Branching statements choose which block runs based on those results.",how:"Use comparisons to express the boundary precisely, combine conditions with && and ||, and make mutually exclusive cases easy to read.",code:"#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int score{82};\n    if (score >= 90) std::cout << \"A\";\n    else if (score >= 60) std::cout << \"Pass\";\n    else std::cout << \"Retry\";\n}",task:"Write a grade classifier with at least four score ranges and test values exactly on every boundary.",q:"What does the && operator mean?",opts:["Both operands must be true","At least one operand must be true","The operand is inverted","The values are compared for equality"],a:0},
{about:"Loops repeat a block while a condition or range requires more work. The loop form determines when the condition is checked and how iteration advances.",how:"Establish an invariant, ensure progress toward termination, and test zero, one, and multiple iterations. Prefer range-based for when visiting every element.",code:"#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int total{};\n    for (int value = 1; value <= 5; ++value) {\n        total += value;\n    }\n    std::cout << total << '\\n';\n}",task:"Calculate the sum of all even numbers from 1 through 100. Explain why your loop always terminates.",q:"When is a do-while loop especially useful?",opts:["When the body must run at least once","When the body must never run","Only when iterating over vectors","Only for compile-time loops"],a:0},
{about:"Functions package a task behind a name and parameter list. Their return type communicates the result, and their scope rules control where names are visible.",how:"Design a function around one responsibility. Pass small values by value, larger read-only objects by const reference, and return results instead of hiding them in globals.",code:"#include <iostream>\n\nint calculateDamage(int base, int bonus) {\n    return base + bonus;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << calculateDamage(12, 3) << '\\n';\n}",task:"Split a calculator into separate functions for addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. Decide how division by zero is reported.",q:"What is the purpose of a function's return type?",opts:["It specifies the type of value returned to the caller","It names the source file","It controls how many times the function runs","It makes all parameters global"],a:0},
{about:"Collections store multiple values under one object. Arrays have a fixed size, while std::vector can grow and shrink while managing its storage.",how:"Use std::array for a fixed-size collection and std::vector for a resizable sequence. Remember that valid indices run from zero to size minus one.",code:"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> scores{12, 18, 25};\n    scores.push_back(30);\n    for (int score : scores) std::cout << score << ' ';\n}",task:"Store five level scores in a vector, calculate their average, and safely handle the empty-vector case.",q:"What does vector::push_back do?",opts:["Adds an element to the end","Removes the first element","Sorts the vector","Changes every element"],a:0},
{about:"Every object has an address and a lifetime. References provide aliases, while pointers store addresses and can represent the absence of an object with nullptr.",how:"Prefer references for required objects and pointers when optionality or reseating is needed. Never use an address after the pointed-to object has ended its lifetime.",code:"#include <iostream>\n\nvoid addBonus(int& score) {\n    score += 10;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int score{20};\n    addBonus(score);\n    std::cout << score << '\\n';\n}",task:"Write one function that changes an integer through a reference and another that accepts an optional pointer. Handle nullptr safely.",q:"What does nullptr represent?",opts:["A pointer that does not point to an object","An integer with value zero in every context","A reference without a target","A deleted variable"],a:0},
{about:"User-defined types combine data and meaning. Structs, classes, and enumerations let a program model concepts more clearly than unrelated primitive values.",how:"Group values that belong together, use enum class for named alternatives, and choose member functions when behavior naturally belongs to a type.",code:"#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nenum class State { Ready, Running, Finished };\nstruct Task { std::string name; State state{State::Ready}; };\n\nint main() {\n    Task task{\"Load level\"};\n    std::cout << task.name << '\\n';\n}",task:"Model a game inventory item with a name, weight, and item category. Create and print two instances.",q:"What is a key benefit of enum class?",opts:["Enumerators are scoped and do not implicitly convert to int","It automatically allocates memory","It creates a virtual base class","It stores unlimited strings"],a:0},
{about:"A class defines the state and operations of its objects. Access control protects invariants, and constructors establish a valid initial state.",how:"Keep data private, expose operations that preserve valid values, initialize members in the member-initializer list, and mark read-only member functions const.",code:"#include <iostream>\n\nclass Health {\npublic:\n    explicit Health(int value) : value_{value < 0 ? 0 : value} {}\n    void damage(int amount) { if (amount > 0) value_ -= amount; }\n    int value() const { return value_; }\nprivate:\n    int value_;\n};",task:"Create a Player class with private health and a public takeDamage function that never allows health to become negative.",q:"Why should class data often be private?",opts:["To protect invariants and control how state changes","To make the object impossible to construct","To prevent member functions from accessing it","To force every value to be global"],a:0},
{about:"Object-oriented design is about assigning responsibilities and defining stable interfaces. Composition builds objects from parts; inheritance models substitutable relationships.",how:"Prefer composition when one object simply uses another. Use virtual functions only when callers genuinely need runtime substitution, and give polymorphic base classes virtual destructors.",code:"#include <iostream>\n#include <memory>\n\nstruct Entity {\n    virtual ~Entity() = default;\n    virtual void update() const = 0;\n};\nstruct Enemy : Entity {\n    void update() const override { std::cout << \"Enemy update\\n\"; }\n};",task:"Design a small game entity hierarchy. Identify one relationship that should use composition and one that genuinely needs polymorphism.",q:"Why does a polymorphic base class usually need a virtual destructor?",opts:["So deleting a derived object through a base pointer destroys the full object correctly","So the base class cannot be instantiated","So virtual calls become compile-time calls","So all derived members become public"],a:0},
{about:"The standard library provides containers with different complexity and invalidation guarantees. Iterators let algorithms work across many container types.",how:"Select a container based on access patterns: vector for contiguous sequences, map for ordered key lookup, unordered_map for average constant-time lookup, and deque for efficient ends.",code:"#include <iostream>\n#include <map>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::map<std::string, int> inventory;\n    inventory[\"potion\"] = 4;\n    inventory[\"key\"] = 1;\n    std::cout << inventory.at(\"potion\") << '\\n';\n}",task:"Represent an inventory as a map from item names to quantities. Add, update, find, and remove entries.",q:"Which container keeps keys ordered?",opts:["std::map","std::unordered_map","std::stack","std::vector"],a:0},
{about:"STL algorithms express common operations over iterator ranges. Lambdas provide small callable objects, while ranges can compose lazy transformations.",how:"Use algorithms such as sort, find_if, transform, and count_if to state intent. Ensure comparators define a strict weak ordering and avoid dangling lambda captures.",code:"#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> scores{12, 30, 18, 42};\n    std::sort(scores.begin(), scores.end());\n    auto high = std::count_if(scores.begin(), scores.end(),\n        [](int score) { return score >= 30; });\n    std::cout << high << '\\n';\n}",task:"Sort a list of player scores, then use count_if to count scores above a threshold. Add a lambda that finds the first passing score.",q:"What does a predicate passed to count_if return?",opts:["A bool indicating whether each element matches","A new container","An iterator category","A sorted copy of the range"],a:0},
{about:"Templates parameterize code over types or values. Generic programming moves reusable logic into compile-time constructs while retaining type checking.",how:"Start with the smallest template that solves the problem. Use concepts to express requirements and avoid over-generalizing an interface that only needs one type.",code:"#include <concepts>\n\ntemplate <typename T>\nrequires std::totally_ordered<T>\nT maximum(T left, T right) {\n    return left < right ? right : left;\n}\n\nint main() { return maximum(4, 9); }",task:"Write a function template that returns the smaller of two values. Add a concept or constraint describing the required comparison operation.",q:"What is the main purpose of a function template?",opts:["To generate type-safe functions for different types","To run a function on another thread","To store values in a database","To delay all compilation until runtime"],a:0},
{about:"Resource management connects an object's lifetime to ownership of resources such as memory, files, locks, and sockets. RAII makes cleanup automatic.",how:"Prefer the Rule of Zero: compose members that already manage their resources. Use unique_ptr for exclusive ownership, shared_ptr only for shared lifetime, and weak_ptr to observe shared objects without owning them.",code:"#include <memory>\n#include <iostream>\n\nstruct Texture { explicit Texture(int id) : id{id} {} int id; };\n\nint main() {\n    auto texture = std::make_unique<Texture>(7);\n    std::cout << texture->id << '\\n';\n}",task:"Build a small Resource wrapper using RAII. Explain who owns the resource and what happens when the object leaves scope.",q:"What does unique_ptr express?",opts:["Exclusive ownership of a dynamically managed object","Shared ownership by any number of pointers","A non-owning reference that may dangle","An object with static storage duration"],a:0},
{about:"Modern C++ adds tools that make intent and state more explicit. Features such as auto, structured bindings, optional, variant, and constexpr each solve different problems.",how:"Use type deduction when the initializer makes the type obvious. Use optional for a value that may be absent, variant for one of several alternatives, and string_view for non-owning text views.",code:"#include <optional>\n#include <string_view>\n\nstd::optional<int> parseLevel(std::string_view text) {\n    if (text.empty()) return std::nullopt;\n    return static_cast<int>(text.size());\n}\n\nint main() { auto level = parseLevel(\"forest\"); }",task:"Write a parser that returns std::optional when input is valid and no value when it is empty. Document the lifetime of every string_view you use.",q:"What does std::optional<T> represent?",opts:["A T value that may be absent","A collection of many T values","A runtime-selected base class","A thread-safe shared pointer"],a:0},
{about:"Reliable programs distinguish expected failures from programming errors. Exceptions, assertions, diagnostics, debuggers, and sanitizers each help at different stages.",how:"Validate input at boundaries, throw exceptions when a function cannot fulfill its contract, catch errors where recovery is possible, and use sanitizers to expose undefined behavior.",code:"#include <iostream>\n#include <stdexcept>\n\nint divide(int numerator, int denominator) {\n    if (denominator == 0) throw std::invalid_argument(\"zero denominator\");\n    return numerator / denominator;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    try { std::cout << divide(12, 3); }\n    catch (const std::exception& e) { std::cerr << e.what(); }\n}",task:"Add input validation to a calculator and test division by zero. Use a debugger to inspect the path taken when invalid input is entered.",q:"Where should an exception normally be caught?",opts:["At a level that can make a meaningful recovery decision","Immediately in every function, even if it cannot recover","Only inside main, regardless of program design","Inside the compiler"],a:0},
{about:"Larger C++ projects separate declarations, definitions, and build configuration. Filesystem I/O, modules, libraries, and CMake help organize reproducible programs.",how:"Keep headers focused, put non-inline definitions in source files, avoid violating the One Definition Rule, and let the build system describe targets and dependencies.",code:"// main.cpp\n#include <fstream>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    std::ofstream file(\"score.txt\");\n    if (!file) return 1;\n    file << 125 << '\\n';\n}",task:"Split a small program into a header and source file, then create a CMake target that builds it.",q:"What is the purpose of a header file in a typical C++ project?",opts:["To share declarations between translation units","To execute code before the compiler starts","To replace the linker","To store runtime variables"],a:0},
{about:"Data structures organize information to support operations efficiently. Algorithm analysis describes how resource use grows as input size increases.",how:"Identify the operation that dominates your workload, choose a structure that supports it, and state complexity in terms of input size rather than machine speed.",code:"#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> values{2, 4, 8, 16, 32};\n    bool found = std::binary_search(values.begin(), values.end(), 16);\n    std::cout << std::boolalpha << found << '\\n';\n}",task:"Compare linear search and binary search on sorted data. Explain why binary search requires a sorted range.",q:"What is the time complexity of binary search on a sorted array?",opts:["O(log n)","O(n²)","O(1) for every possible input","O(2ⁿ)"],a:0},
{about:"Concurrent programs run tasks that may overlap in time. Threads, mutexes, condition variables, and atomics coordinate access, but do not remove the need for clear ownership.",how:"Give shared state a synchronization strategy, keep critical sections small, prefer RAII lock guards, and measure performance before adding concurrency.",code:"#include <iostream>\n#include <mutex>\n#include <thread>\n\nint main() {\n    std::mutex output;\n    std::jthread worker([&] {\n        std::lock_guard lock(output);\n        std::cout << \"Worker finished\\n\";\n    });\n}",task:"Create two worker threads that update separate counters, then safely combine the results. Explain why a data race would be undefined behavior.",q:"What problem does a mutex solve?",opts:["It protects shared data from simultaneous unsynchronized access","It makes every function run faster","It automatically divides work between CPU cores","It prevents all exceptions"],a:0}
];
